============================================================================
THE MECHANISM

walkPhase generates a rule body for every grammar rule and installs it. Each
body compiles perfectly on its own. Once they are all installed, every one of
them fails to compile with the same signature -- reached end of input, on line
one -- as though the body text were empty.

WHAT IS WRONG

fixBisect narrowed that to a SINGLE install. With the first forty two bodies
installed, the first body still fails on its CONTENT, so its text is being
read. Install one more -- the forty third, the tokenizer hook -- and the same
body, on the same binary, fails as an empty read.

That left a confound: forty three installs, or THAT install? The obvious
control was to skip the suspect by name, and it was VOID -- two spellings both
matched every member and installed nothing, so they said nothing either way.
It is now bear-trap 28's fourth row: an eq against a tag inside an iterate body
matches universally.

WHAT THIS FILE DOES INSTEAD -- a same-count swap, and why it had to be one

The eligible population is exactly forty three bodies and tokenize is the LAST
of them, so forty three installs WITHOUT tokenize cannot be built. Substitution
is impossible at that count. So both arms run at FORTY TWO installs and differ
only in which member is present:

    arm 0   the first forty two, tokenize absent      CONTENT read
    arm 1   BlocK dropped, tokenize present instead   EMPTY read

Same number of installs. One member exchanged for another. Opposite outcomes.

WHY tokenize, AND NOT SOME OTHER RULE -- CANDIDATE-GRADE, and read the grade

⚠ IT IS NOT COMPILED TWICE. That reading is available from the evidence and it
is wrong, so it is named here to be shot: nothing about install 43 compiles
anything a second time. Ruling on double-compilation would send the fix at the
wrong target entirely.

The candidate is a DUAL-ROLE COLLISION. tokenize wears two hats at once.

    hat one    it is citizen 43 of the population -- a grammar rule like any
               other, so the campaign dutifully generates a parse body for it
               and installs it, exactly as it does for the other forty two
    hat two    it is the tokenizer HOOK, grammar line 34, the live machinery
               the reading side calls to turn raw text into tokens

The install does not break tokenize-the-rule. It breaks tokenize-the-HOOK.
After install 43, when the sweep goes to read BasicElse's text, the read routes
through a body whose CodE now says how to PARSE THE tokenize RULE instead of
how to TOKENIZE. That yields no tokens from the raw text, so the very first
read reports end of input at line one.

That is the signature, and it is the one double-compilation cannot produce:
not tokenize failing, but EVERYTHING AFTER THE INSTALL BECOMING UNREADABLE
while each individual body stays perfect. Individually perfect, collectively
unreadable -- because the collective's reading channel was one of the
individuals.

WHAT IS MEASURED, AND WHAT IS NOT

    measured     the same-count swap above. Forty two installs either way, one
                 member exchanged, opposite answers. That isolates the install.
    measured     the install re-points nothing. showBody is identical before
                 and after; what changes is CodE overwritten and isCodeD set
                 INSIDE the one body every reader already shares.
    structural   tokenize is the tokenizer hook at grammar line 34, used by
                 NamE, NumbeR, HeX and FormaT.
    NOT measured NOBODY HAS WATCHED A READ ACTUALLY ENTER THE OVERWRITTEN
                 BODY. The whole causal story above rests on that step and it
                 has never been observed.

THE ONE MEASUREMENT THAT WOULD PROMOTE IT from candidate to established: trace
a single read after install 43 and watch it enter tokenize's overwritten body.
Until someone does that, the story here is a candidate and is labelled one.

WHAT IT RHYMES WITH, offered as orientation and not as evidence

The off-rule storage and explicit-activation ruling already carries three
customers -- the napalm, the BlocK re-poison, and mid-walk setParse binding --
and all three are variants of installing over live machinery while the
machinery is still in use. On this mechanism tokenize is the fourth. Two fix
SHAPES follow, and neither is recommended here because the mechanism is not
established: generate tokenize's body like everyone else, store it off-rule,
and activate only when nothing further needs the old tokenizer; or exempt the
hook from installation entirely, which is a ruling about whether tokenize
should ever self-host its own parse.

⚠ NOTE THE ABSENCE OF A REMEDY BLOCK, AND THAT IT IS DELIBERATE. Addendum 3
clause 3: an absent REMEDY block means the issue is genuinely open, and a
speculative remedy must never be written as though recommended. The two shapes
above are candidates carrying their grade, in diagnosis prose, which is where
clause 3 puts them. A REMEDY block gets written here when the mechanism is
established and a fix is chosen -- not before.

THE RULING WANTED

Confirm or refute, from the output above. Measured 2026-08-20 it CONFIRMS: the
poisoning follows tokenize's identity and not the count. What is owed is the
architect's word on that, because everything gated on parse generation closing
sits behind it.

WHAT THE POINTERS SAY, which is the second half of the done-when

showBody prints the suspect before and after the installs and the two lines are
IDENTICAL -- same node, same groupBody. So the install does not RE-POINT the
field. It overwrites the CodE inside the one body every reader already shares,
and sets isCodeD on it. The field the install clobbers is Grokking tokenize
itself, in place. That is why a later read tokenizes nothing.

HOW TO USE THIS FILE

Run it. It names the one body it left out, so a skip that silently skips
nothing cannot be mistaken for a result -- which is exactly how the last
control was lost. The anchor fixF31Here sits where the verdict line has just
been printed; a breakpoint conditioned on that name lands with the answer on
screen. The other arm is one character: set f31Arm to zero.

